# Threat-detection (TD) rules for
# fw-intent-app-layer-protocol-negated-unknown-shortcircuit (,
# 4.13, 11.3).
# These rules exercise the negated+unknown short-circuit behavior of
# `DetectAppLayerProtocolPacketMatch`. Both rules carry negated
# app-layer-protocol keywords that should NEVER fire alerts on this
# PCAP because:
# 1. During the Detection_Window (flow->alproto == ALPROTO_UNKNOWN):
# the short-circuit returns 0 immediately — no match, no alert.
# 2. After classification (flow->alproto == ALPROTO_TLS): the NOR
# check fails because TLS is in the negated list — no match.
# The expected alert count for BOTH rules is ZERO across all packets.
# Rule design:
# sid:9001 — single-value negated: `app-layer-protocol:!tls;`
# Short-circuits during Detection_Window (negated + unknown).
# After classification to TLS: !tls → FALSE (TLS matches
# the negated value).
# sid:9002 — multi-value negated: `app-layer-protocol:!tls,http;`
# Same short-circuit during Detection_Window.
# After classification to TLS: NOR(tls, http) → FALSE
# because TLS is in the list.
# Both rules use `flow:established` to focus on the post-handshake
# packets where app-layer probing is active. The handshake packets
# (SYN/SYN-ACK/ACK) do not carry application data and are less
# interesting for this test — the short-circuit still applies to them,
# but using `flow:established` narrows the test surface to the
# Detection_Window boundary where the behavior matters most.

# Single-value negated form (baseline behavior, preserved)
alert tcp any any <> any any (msg:"negated single-value !tls during detection window"; flow:established; app-layer-protocol:!tls; sid:9001;)

# Multi-value negated form (new in this feature, )
alert tcp any any <> any any (msg:"negated multi-value !tls,http during detection window"; flow:established; app-layer-protocol:!tls,http; sid:9002;)
